      "text": "The numerator and denominator could be factored as \\((a + bi)\\), \\((a - bi)\\) respectively, where a and b are numbers. Hence;\n\\[\\frac{3 + 5i}{3 - 5i} = \\frac{(a + bi)(b + ai)}{(a - bi)(b + ai)} = \\frac{(x + yi)^2}{\\text{squared unit circle radius}}.\\]\nNote that we are used radicands $\\sqrt{(x^2 - y^2) + 2xyi}, \\sqrt{(x^2 - y^2) - 2xyi}.$\nLet's find the radii of the square unitcicles created by rewriting the numerator and denominator. The numerator will have unit radius $(x, y)$ with $(x^2 + y^2) = 9$.\nSimilarly, the denominator has unit radius $(x, y)$ where $(x^2 + y^2) = 9$.\nNotice that $-2xy$ cancels out, leaving only $9x^2 = 27$, so $x = \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{3}} = \\pm\\sqrt{3}$ and $y = \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{3}} = \\pm\\sqrt{3}$.\nFurthermore, $(\\sqrt{3} - \\sqrt{3})$ cancel out and so will $i \\sqrt{3}$.\nWriting our square unitcicles we have,\n\\[1) \\quad (0, 0), \\quad (\\sqrt{3}, \\sqrt{3}), \\quad (-\\sqrt{3}, \\sqrt{3}), \\quad (\\sqrt{3}, -\\sqrt{3}), \\quad (-\\sqrt{3}, -\\sqrt{3}),\\]\n\\[2) \\quad (0, 0), \\quad (\\sqrt{3}, -\\sqrt{3}), \\quad (-\\sqrt{3}, -\\sqrt{3}), \\quad (\\sqrt{3}, \\sqrt{3}), \\quad (-\\sqrt{3}, \\sqrt{3}), \\quad (\\sqrt{3}, -\\sqrt{3}), \\quad (-\\sqrt{3}, -\\sqrt{3}),\\]\n\\[3) \\quad (0, 0), \\quad (0, i\\sqrt{3}), \\quad (0, -i\\sqrt{3}), \\quad (i\\sqrt{3}, 0), \\quad (-i\\sqrt{3}, 0),\\]\nHaving paired - similar real and imaginary items up, our system has one double root at $(0, 0)$ and all other roots happen to come in pairs, giving us the sum of these roots as $0$.\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{0}$. The answer is 0",
